WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.700
There are two ways to get work out of a model.

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.835
Let's give both the same task: write this week's summary.

3
00:00:06.265 --> 00:00:10.462
The agent gets a goal and decides its own steps. It reads. It thinks.

4
00:00:10.492 --> 00:00:12.299
It tries something. It checks.

5
00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:16.525
That freedom is why agents are good at building things.

6
00:00:16.555 --> 00:00:19.725
It's also why two runs can come out different.

7
00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:24.647
The pipeline gets steps that you chose. Plan. Write. Check.

8
00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:27.484
Each one is a single, narrow request.

9
00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:31.683
Every run takes the same path. You can test each step.

10
00:00:31.713 --> 00:00:35.764
And you can use a cheaper model wherever it's good enough.

11
00:00:36.194 --> 00:00:39.660
And when a check fails, the work isn't thrown away.

12
00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.333
It's set aside for a person to look at.

13
00:00:42.763 --> 00:00:45.512
So. Use an agent to build the pipeline.

14
00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:48.577
Use the pipeline for the work that repeats.
